{"title":"How plants make food using sunlight, water and air","key_idea":"Plants use sunlight energy, water from roots, and carbon dioxide from air to make their own food in their leaves.","method":["Sunlight enters the leaf and is trapped by the green chlorophyll in the leaf cells.","Water travels up from the roots through tubes in the stem to reach the leaves.","Carbon dioxide enters the leaf through tiny holes called stomata on the leaf surface.","The plant uses the sunlight energy to combine water and carbon dioxide together to make glucose (food) and release oxygen.","The plant keeps the glucose to grow and use as energy, and releases oxygen into the air for animals and humans to breathe."],"worked_example":{"problem":"A plant leaf receives sunlight, has water in its cells, and stomata are open. What happens inside the leaf?","steps":["Chlorophyll in the leaf traps the sunlight energy.","Water from the roots and carbon dioxide from the air meet inside the leaf.","Using the sunlight energy, the water and carbon dioxide combine to form glucose.","Oxygen is released from the leaf into the air."],"answer":"The leaf makes glucose food for the plant and releases oxygen for breathing."},"key_terms":[{"term":"Chlorophyll","meaning":"The green substance in leaves that captures sunlight energy."},{"term":"Stomata","meaning":"Tiny pores on the leaf surface through which carbon dioxide enters."},{"term":"Glucose","meaning":"The sugar food that plants make during photosynthesis."},{"term":"Producers","meaning":"Plants that make their own food instead of eating other things."}],"common_mistakes":["Thinking plants eat food from the soil instead of making their own food.","Forgetting that plants need all three things—sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide—together to make food."],"remember":"Plants need sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to make food in their leaves and release oxygen.","quick_check":[{"q":"Where does the carbon dioxide that a plant uses come from?","a":"It comes from the air and enters through stomata on the leaf."},{"q":"Why are plants called producers?","a":"Because they make their own food using sunlight, water and carbon dioxide."}],"tell_your_child":"Plants are like little factories in the leaves that turn sunlight, water and air into food. The green colour in leaves is what catches the sunlight and makes all this magic happen!","picture":{"concept_key":"c5-science-photosynthesis","scene_prompt":"A sunny garden with a green leafy plant growing from soil, water droplets on roots, and a child smiling nearby watching the plant."}}
```
